 "title": "How to Tell a Story",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains that telling stories in the past tense causes audiences to lose interest. The main lesson is to switch to the present tense so listeners can experience the moment in real-time.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never tell your stories in the past tense.",
  "Tell the story in the present tense to keep the audience on the edge of their seat.",
  "Present tense makes the moment feel like it is unfolding as you speak.",
  "The audience gets to experience what happens next right there with you in real time.",
  "Practice by taking a story you already tell and changing phrases like \"I was\" to \"I am\"."
 ],
 "content": "The most simple switch you can make to become a better storyteller (it works in SECONDS!) 👀 Never tell stories like this. If you tell your stories in this way, half of the room is going to zone out and pick up their phones. But if you make this one little change that I'm about to tell you, you will have the entire room on the edge of their seat and in the palm of your hand. First, if we're just meeting, hi, I'm Jen. I help entrepreneurs build profitable personal brands through stages and social media. I have helped hundreds of entrepreneurs become powerful communicators, and this is one of the first changes that I teach them to make. Here it is. Tell the story in present tense. Let me give you an example. Listen to the difference. So yesterday I went to the grocery store and I bumped into my ex-boyfriend. I tried to leave before he saw me, but... Now try this. I'm walking through the grocery store. I turn down the cereal aisle, and there's my ex-boyfriend. I turn the cart around, but then I hear, \"Jen!\" When you tell a story in present tense, it feels like the moment is unfolding as you speak. Your audience gets to experience what happens next right there with you in real time. Here's how you can practice this. Tell a story you already tell and change \"I was\" to \"I am.\" Try it out in your next video, on a stage, or over dinner tonight, and follow for more simple ways to become a top communicator. Text on screen: The worst way to tell a story (And how to fix it!), I'm Jen! [Image of Instagram profile showing Jen Gottlieb, Keynote Speaker], [Video clip of Jen speaking on stage and at a workshop], ❌❌❌ Past tense, ✔️✔️✔️ Present tense, I was..., I am.",
